course: medicine
lesson: 25
title: The GP, the family doctor and the nurse
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the UK your first stop for most health problems is the GP, the general practitioner or family doctor, who knows a little about everything and sends you to a specialist when needed.
explain: At the surgery a doctor is like a detective: the clues are your answers, the sounds in your chest, the look of your ears and throat, and sometimes a blood test.
explain: That's why a good doctor asks you, not just your mum: only you know exactly where it hurts and what it feels like.
explain: The practice nurse is a trained professional too, who gives vaccines, dresses wounds and checks children with asthma or diabetes.
fact: A GP is a general practitioner, or family doctor, who looks after all kinds of health problems and refers people to specialists if needed.
fact: Receptionists ask what the problem is so they can find the right appointment and spot urgent cases first, sometimes with a nurse or pharmacist or by phone.
fact: A stethoscope lets the doctor hear the heart and lungs, and wheezes or crackles can be signs of illness.
fact: Doctors look in ears with an otoscope and in the throat with a light and a flat wooden stick, and saying «aah» helps them see.
fact: A child's height and weight are plotted on a growth chart to check they are growing well, and being a different size from friends is usually normal.
fact: Good doctors talk to children directly, because children often know best how they feel.
fact: A blood test takes only a little blood from the arm or hand, through a small needle, often after numbing cream, and the body replaces it quickly.
fact: Blood tests can check things like iron, infections and how organs are working.
fact: A specialist is a doctor who focuses on one part of the body or one kind of illness, and being referred doesn't mean something terrible is wrong.
fact: Some appointments happen by phone or video when no examination is needed, and the doctor decides if you must come in.
fact: In the USA and many other countries children often see a paediatrician, a children's doctor, for everyday care; in the UK children usually see a GP and meet paediatricians in hospital.
exercise: What is a GP? | A family doctor who deals with all kinds of problems | A doctor who only treats bones | A hospital surgeon | A nurse who gives jabs
exercise: Why does the receptionist ask what's wrong? | To find the right appointment and spot urgent cases | To gossip about patients | To decide the price | To check you're not lying just to get a quick appointment
exercise: What does the doctor listen to with a stethoscope? | The heart and lungs | Your thoughts | Your tummy rumbling only | Your bones
exercise: What sounds in the chest can be signs of illness? | Wheezes or crackles | A steady heartbeat | Quiet breathing | Nothing, chests are silent
exercise: Why does the doctor ask you to say «aah»? | It helps them see your throat | It warms up the cold stethoscope | It tests how well you can sing | It stops a cough from starting up
exercise: What tool does a doctor use to look in ears? | An otoscope | A stethoscope | A thermometer | A microscope
exercise: Why does the nurse weigh and measure you? | To check you're growing well on a growth chart | To decide which school you will be allowed to go to | To see if you are too greedy | To pick your football team
exercise: Why does the doctor ask you questions, not only Mum? | You often know best how you feel | Mum is not allowed to answer any questions | You are in trouble | Doctors don't trust parents
exercise: What can a blood test check? | Iron, infections and how organs work | Your favourite food and favourite colour | How clever you are | Your eye colour
exercise: Does a blood test take a lot of blood? | No, a little, and it's quickly replaced | Yes, about a bottle | Yes, so much that you can't play for a month | Half your blood
exercise: What is a specialist? | A doctor who focuses on one part of the body or kind of illness | A doctor who knows everything | A doctor for animals | A nurse in training
exercise: Does a referral to a specialist mean something terrible is wrong? | No, it often just needs a closer look | Yes, always | Yes, it always means a big operation is coming | Only on Mondays
exercise: When can an appointment be by video call? | When no examination is needed | Never, it's not allowed by the rules | Only for checking on broken legs | Only at night, when the surgery is shut
exercise: Who gives most vaccinations at the GP surgery? | The practice nurse | The receptionist | The pharmacist from next door | The child's teacher
exercise: What is a paediatrician? | A doctor who specialises in children | A foot doctor | A doctor for teeth | A doctor who looks after old people only
test: "nurses are just doctors' helpers" — right? | No, they're trained professionals with their own work | Yes, they only fetch things | Yes, they can't give jabs | No, they're doctors in training
test: Your cousin in Ohio says he sees "a pediatrician" for a cough. In England you'd usually see... | A GP | A dentist | A surgeon | A pharmacist only
test: Doctor asks if you've been weeing a lot. You're embarrassed. Best move? | Answer honestly, it's a clue | Say nothing and look at the floor | Make something up to sound normal | Let Mum guess the answer for you
test: "the stethoscope is cold cos doctors are mean" lol. Real reason? | Metal is cold, and they need it on your skin to hear | Doctors chill it on purpose | Cold helps hearing | It's kept in a fridge
test: You're smaller than all your friends. What would the growth chart show? | Whether you're growing steadily, usually normal | That you're ill | That you definitely eat far too little at mealtimes | Nothing useful
test: What would happen if the GP didn't refer anyone? | Problems needing a specialist might be missed | Everyone would get better faster | Specialists would come and visit everyone's homes instead | GPs would become surgeons
test: Mum on hold: "why does she need to know what's wrong, just give me a slot". Best answer? | The receptionist sorts urgent cases and the right person | It's the law to be nosy | She decides the medicine | She sends the bill
test: Which visit could be a video call? | Talking about a long-term rash with photos | Checking a chest for crackles with a stethoscope | Taking a blood test | Looking in your ears
test: "blood test = they take loads and you faint" — truth? | They take a little, it's soon replaced, and you can lie down | They take a litre | You must faint | It hurts for days
test: Doctor drama on TV: doctor knows what's wrong just by looking. Real life? | Doctors gather clues: questions, examination and tests | Doctors always know at a glance | Doctors guess | Machines decide everything alone
test: Which is a job for the practice nurse? | Asthma reviews and vaccinations | Doing brain surgery on the patients | Writing the children's school reports | Fixing teeth and filling holes in them
exam: Identify the professional who, within the United Kingdom, ordinarily serves as the first point of contact for health concerns of a general character. | The general practitioner | The consultant hospital surgeon | The orthodontist | The paramedic
exam: Ascertain the instrument by means of which a physician auscultates the heart and lungs. | The stethoscope | The otoscope | The thermometer | The sphygmograph
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning referral to a specialist. | It does not of itself signify a grave condition | It invariably signifies a grave condition | It is permitted only to adults | It precludes further consultation with the general practitioner
exam: Determine the principal purpose of plotting a child's height and weight upon a growth chart. | To ascertain whether growth is proceeding satisfactorily | To determine eligibility for school admission | To fix the cost of treatment | To select a sporting discipline
